00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:52.080
There is Oracle, this year it laid off 21,000&nbsp;
workers to build its business around AI.&nbsp;

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:56.080
They estimate that a layoff this large&nbsp;
could free up $8 billion to $10 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:01.120
for AI infrastructure spending.
But, Oracle is particularly painful.&nbsp;

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:06.720
Some employees had their bonuses tied to&nbsp;
unvested restricted stock, which was now gone.&nbsp;

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:11.520
One former software manager lost nearly $1&nbsp;
million in unvested stock that he was set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:16.720
to get in just four months, despite&nbsp;
it making up 70% of his total pay.&nbsp;

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:22.160
A technical writer also lost a $300,000&nbsp;
bonus tied to unvested restricted stock.&nbsp;

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:24.960
It sure seemed like Oracle&nbsp;
was targeting these layoffs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:29.760
at people with outstanding stock.
As a 30-year Oracle veteran said

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:34.640
“It seems (BUT I DON’T KNOW), maybe,&nbsp;
layoffs follow an algorithm of high level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:40.320
individual contributors and mid-level managers –&nbsp;
especially those with outstanding stock options,”&nbsp;

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:46.960
For context, Oracle just reported its best growth&nbsp;
quarter in 15 years, now with a market cap over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:53.680
$400 billion. Though, Oracle is experiencing&nbsp;
some pretty big problems with its AI plans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:56.880
as we’ll find out soon.
But in the meantime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:00.640
workers are having a rough time.
Some ex-staff were in the U.S. on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:06.480
H-1B visas and now had just 60 days to&nbsp;
find another job or leave the country.&nbsp;

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:10.880
Many of the layoffs are those over&nbsp;
40 years old, and 22% are those who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:15.920
worked at Oracle for over 15 years.
Workers demanded Oracle match the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:20.960
generous severance packages offered by Meta,&nbsp;
Microsoft, and Cloudflare, including longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:25.920
pay and accelerated stock vesting.
Yeah, Oracle didn’t do that.&nbsp;

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:31.040
But there wasn't much employees could do about it.
Oracle employees aren't unionised.&nbsp;

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:36.320
In April, more than 600 Oracle employees signed a&nbsp;
letter to company management asking for increased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:41.040
severance, extended health care, and other&nbsp;
benefits. Oracle responded by saying that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:44.400
will only address concerns individually.
Not only that,&nbsp;

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:48.320
“The company has since been rejecting many&nbsp;
individual requests for better severance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:51.200
sometimes responding with cookie-cutter&nbsp;
language that has nothing to do with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:56.080
the individual’s circumstances,”.
Oh, Oracle workers were training the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:00.560
AI that replaced them, by the way.
The job cuts were painful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:06.640
but what made them particularly bad was how these&nbsp;
executives chose to… justify?... or label them?

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:11.440
The founder of Oracle, Larry Ellison,&nbsp;
bragged at an Oracle development conference:

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:16.800
But in reality, former employees had to spend&nbsp;
time trying to reverse-engineer faulty code that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:21.520
juniors had generated due to Oracle’s AI mandates.
Ellison also said:&nbsp;

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:27.680
"We are choosing the chips. Anyone whose job is&nbsp;
not making the chips run faster... is at risk."&nbsp;

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:30.960
Workers are basically training&nbsp;
an AI clone of themselves.&nbsp;

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:33.520
"They're having you do something, it's recorded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:36.800
and then they're going to replace&nbsp;
you with whatever you just built."&nbsp;

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:41.200
Some CEOs justify job cuts by&nbsp;
undermining the people they just fired.&nbsp;

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:44.960
Zuckerberg described the layoffs&nbsp;
as removing “low performers.”&nbsp;

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:49.600
Pretty awful thing to be labelled when trying&nbsp;
to find new work, especially when many of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:53.840
these employees weren’t low performers&nbsp;
at all, according to internal reviews.&nbsp;

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:58.960
Cloudflare CEO Matthew Prince also sorted&nbsp;
staff into "measurers" vs builders and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:03.200
wrote that "the vast majority of those&nbsp;
we laid off last week were measurers".&nbsp;

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:07.200
“The way these layoffs have been handled,&nbsp;
it feels like a clear signal that humans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:12.000
are treated like another asset. Just like&nbsp;
data center assets that can be disposed of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:18.000
if they’re not the most profitable option.”
But, some of these layoffs are backfiring.&nbsp;

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:22.960
When things are done in such bad faith,&nbsp;
there can often be large blowback.

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:32.720
These layoffs are pretty depressing, but,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:38.240
we’ve gotten some pretty funny responses from&nbsp;
staff. Those cut, and even those who weren’t.

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:42.640
After announcing layoffs, two engineers at&nbsp;
Pinterest built a tool to track internal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:46.240
job cuts after the company refused&nbsp;
to share information identifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:52.640
impacted employees. They were quickly fired.
The CEO told workers at a company-wide meeting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:57.120
“they should consider working elsewhere if they&nbsp;
are working against the direction of the company,”&nbsp;

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:01.680
We're seeing a shift in corporate culture,&nbsp;
and things only get more dramatic from here.&nbsp;

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:06.240
Workers' loyalty to their companies is&nbsp;
essentially gone. But loyalty to fellow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:12.000
workers and co-workers is growing stronger.
A perfect example is Vasilios Syrakis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:17.520
who was laid off in March 2026, after spending&nbsp;
more than eight years as a senior systems engineer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:22.640
working on Atlassian's edge infrastructure.
He was part of the company's more than 1,600&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:28.080
layoffs as it redirected spending towards AI.
So he released a video.

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:37.520
Within just eight days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:42.720
this video had passed 1 million views.&nbsp;
It currently sits at over 2.3 million.

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:47.280
“This has to be the most badass thing&nbsp;
ever to happen in the IT world this year”&nbsp;

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:50.720
“Build infrastructure for company&nbsp;
to grow to nearly $25 billion market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:52.960
cap ($1.79 billion in revenue last&nbsp;
quarter). Gets laid off. Gives away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:57.440
Atlassian's entire infrastructure&nbsp;
for free online for anyone to copy.”&nbsp;

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:02.480
He’s perfectly within his rights to do this, by&nbsp;
the way. Most of it was publicly available on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:05.600
Atlassian's engineering blog.
Videos like this are now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:10.080
popping up all over YouTube.
But some videos are a bit different.

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:14.160
This is David Frenk, who was laid&nbsp;
off. He made this American Pie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:18.080
parody for other Meta employees,&nbsp;
which then leaked outside of Meta.&nbsp;

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:22.560
Also, remember the weird letter&nbsp;
from the Cloudflare CEO? Well…&nbsp;

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:27.280
This is Chamath Palihapitiya, venture&nbsp;
capitalist and former Facebook executive.

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:21.520
He’s totally right though.
I think these executives are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:25.360
scared to be honest about why they’re doing&nbsp;
these layoffs, which is basically putting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.360 --> 00:12:30.720
it back into AI spend, so they come up with&nbsp;
terms like “measurers”, or “low performers”.

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:36.480
I don’t know if layoffs will stop, but&nbsp;
it does seem like CEOs are realising just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:40.960
laying everyone off isn’t helping a ton.
Zuckerberg has also admitted that he made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:45.760
mistakes with some of these layoffs.
Now Meta is trying to win workers back.&nbsp;

00:12:45.760 --> 00:12:49.760
The company plans to increase spending&nbsp;
on offsites and corporate events,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:54.640
including a company-wide hackathon in July.
And all of this is happening while Meta&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.640 --> 00:13:00.160
raises its annual spending forecast&nbsp;
from $125 billion to $145 billion.&nbsp;

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:03.040
I think he knows what these&nbsp;
layoffs have cost them.&nbsp;

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:08.560
Not just in capital but in trust.
Ford is also re-hiring 300 veteran&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:14.400
quality inspectors, after the AI that replaced&nbsp;
them… didn’t really live up to expectations.&nbsp;

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:16.360
Who could’ve seen that coming.
[Charles Poon, vice president&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.360 --> 00:13:18.880
of vehicle hardware engineering, Ford]:&nbsp;
“Over prior years, we didn't pay as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:23.520
attention as we should have to the experience&nbsp;
of our most knowledgeable engineers that have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:28.640
been with us through many product cycles”
Wish more executives would admit this fact.

00:13:28.640 --> 00:13:32.640
But, not everyone is interested in reconciliation.&nbsp;

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:38.320
Some workers have chosen a very different&nbsp;
response: revenge. Even to the point of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.320 --> 00:13:43.360
sabotaging their own careers.
And this is only increasing.

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:54.480
According to a Fortune survey, 29% of&nbsp;
employees admitted to sabotaging their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:59.920
company's AI strategy. Among Gen Z&nbsp;
workers, that figure jumps to 44%.&nbsp;

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:06.640
Some engage in what is known as data poisoning,&nbsp;
deliberately feeding AI systems bad, misleading,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:10.080
or proprietary information&nbsp;
to contaminate the output.&nbsp;

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:15.040
Others manipulate performance metrics, making&nbsp;
it appear that AI tools are performing worse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.040 --> 00:14:20.480
than they actually are. Roughly one in&nbsp;
ten workers admit to doing exactly that.&nbsp;

00:14:20.480 --> 00:14:25.520
Some bypass the company AI system to feed&nbsp;
their company data to other AI platforms.&nbsp;

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:28.960
Then there is what some call generating garbage.&nbsp;

00:14:28.960 --> 00:14:35.040
Workers intentionally prompt AI systems to produce&nbsp;
low-quality, nonsensical, or unusable results,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:40.400
creating the impression that the technology&nbsp;
is unreliable or simply not ready for the job.

00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:44.960
There is a growing tension&nbsp;
between employers and employees.&nbsp;

00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:49.760
And as companies push deeper into&nbsp;
AI, trust appears to be collapsing.&nbsp;

00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:55.120
A Yahoo survey found that 67% of respondents&nbsp;
believed their company had already suffered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.120 --> 00:15:00.320
a data leak or security breach because&nbsp;
an employee used an unapproved AI tool.&nbsp;

00:15:00.320 --> 00:15:05.040
At the same time, 76% of executives&nbsp;
said employee sabotage poses a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.040 --> 00:15:10.560
serious threat to their company's future.
And perhaps the reason isn't hard to find.&nbsp;

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:16.800
In the same survey, 60% of companies said they&nbsp;
plan to lay off workers who refuse to adopt AI.&nbsp;

00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:23.040
Think about the dynamic that creates.
Use AI, or get laid off. And even if you do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:27.840
you might still get laid off. Doesn't&nbsp;
make you feel great no matter what you do.&nbsp;

00:15:27.840 --> 00:15:32.400
How can you work in an environment like this?
So many are trying to “poison” the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:36.240
AI that’s replacing them.
Several AI-industry insiders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.240 --> 00:15:41.280
launched a project called Poison Fountain.
Its goal is simple: convincing website&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:45.840
operators to embed poisoned information&nbsp;
designed specifically for AI crawlers.&nbsp;

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:51.680
Another survey of 1,000 employees and&nbsp;
178 managers found that 85% of recently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.680 --> 00:15:56.560
laid-off workers attempted some form of&nbsp;
retaliation against their former employer.&nbsp;

00:15:56.560 --> 00:16:01.440
Remote workers were 47% more likely&nbsp;
to do so than on-site employees.&nbsp;

00:16:01.440 --> 00:16:06.720
The most common methods were surprisingly simple:&nbsp;
posting inappropriate content on company social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:11.520
media accounts, sending inappropriate emails&nbsp;
to clients, and stealing company assets.&nbsp;

00:16:11.520 --> 00:16:17.040
Meanwhile, DTEX's Insider Risk report&nbsp;
found a 35% increase in data theft by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:22.560
departing employees during layoff events.
That was back in 2023 and it’s likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.560 --> 00:16:27.840
much higher now, and we’re seeing it,&nbsp;
especially in some very extreme cases.

00:16:27.840 --> 00:16:33.040
Tech companies are increasingly finding&nbsp;
themselves in legal battles with former employees.&nbsp;

00:16:33.040 --> 00:16:38.720
After Intel announced more than 35,000 job&nbsp;
cuts over two years, the company accused Luo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.720 --> 00:16:43.840
a software engineer of 11 years, of copying&nbsp;
sensitive company files before disappearing.&nbsp;

00:16:43.840 --> 00:16:47.600
According to Intel, he spent four&nbsp;
days transferring more than 18,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:52.160
documents, some marked "Intel Top&nbsp;
Secret," onto an external drive.&nbsp;

00:16:52.160 --> 00:16:55.440
Then, he was gone.
We don’t condone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.440 --> 00:17:00.720
this… of course… but that’s pretty funny.
Intel’s spent more than three months trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:04.800
to contact him through phone calls,&nbsp;
emails, and even physical letters.&nbsp;

00:17:04.800 --> 00:17:10.000
The company is now suing for $250,000&nbsp;
and demanding the return of the data.&nbsp;

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:14.240
His whereabouts remain unknown.
And Intel isn't alone.&nbsp;

00:17:14.240 --> 00:17:19.440
Elon Musk's xAI has also gone after&nbsp;
a former engineer, Xuechen Li.&nbsp;

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:25.120
Allegedly, Li took confidential information,&nbsp;
including uploading xAI's entire codebase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.120 --> 00:17:28.560
before moving to OpenAI.
A judge temporarily blocked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:34.160
him from working on or discussing AI technology&nbsp;
with OpenAI while the case moved through court.&nbsp;

00:17:34.160 --> 00:17:38.480
According to xAI, this wasn't just&nbsp;
one employee taking information.&nbsp;

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:43.680
The company claims OpenAI was actively recruiting&nbsp;
workers with access to Grok's source code.

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:48.240
It feels like something’s broken right now.
Socially, that is.&nbsp;

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:53.680
Workers gave companies their time, loyalty,&nbsp;
expertise, and sometimes entire careers.&nbsp;

00:17:53.680 --> 00:17:57.280
In return, companies offered&nbsp;
stability, opportunity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.280 --> 00:18:01.600
career growth, and the promise that if&nbsp;
you performed well, you’d be rewarded.&nbsp;

00:18:01.600 --> 00:18:06.560
This was the social, unspoken&nbsp;
contract. It goes both ways.&nbsp;

00:18:06.560 --> 00:18:10.480
But doesn’t it feel like that&nbsp;
unspoken trust is breaking?&nbsp;

00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:14.480
Oracle clawed back stock that employees&nbsp;
had spent years working toward.&nbsp;

00:18:14.480 --> 00:18:18.480
Meta pushed out workers while branding&nbsp;
many of them as "low performers."&nbsp;

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:22.240
Cloudflare calling staff "measurers."
4am layoff emails,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.240 --> 00:18:28.080
years of loyalty erased overnight.
Why work here, if there’s no mutual benefit?&nbsp;

00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:32.880
I think it’s why people are spending&nbsp;
less time with companies. Because, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:39.040
they don’t take care of them like they used to.
A company has moved away from a group of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.040 --> 00:18:46.480
people who agree to work towards a mutual goal.
Now it is just AI, executives and shareholders.

00:18:46.480 --> 00:18:49.760
This is just the tip of the iceberg&nbsp;
though. Companies are trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.760 --> 00:18:54.960
replace workers everywhere, and one&nbsp;
of the big ones is customer support.&nbsp;

00:18:54.960 --> 00:18:59.760
But, in a lot of ways, it’s&nbsp;
backfiring, in some pretty funny ways.

00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:02.400
To learn how, watch this video next.

